    "content": "### Introduction: The Dual Legal Regimes of Commercial Flight\n\nWhen a commercial flight is delayed by seven hours, when a checked suitcase containing expensive business attire disappears into an airport baggage carousel void, or when an international flight is canceled on the tarmac at [Toronto Pearson (YYZ)](/airport/YYZ), what legal rules govern passenger compensation?\n\nTo most travelers, consumer protection appears straightforward: filing a claim on the airline's website or lodging a complaint with the Canadian Transportation Agency (CTA). However, commercial aviation operates under a complex hierarchy of international treaty law and domestic consumer regulations. On international itineraries, passenger rights are anchored by an unshakeable United Nations multilateral treaty: the **Montreal Convention of 1999 (MC99)**.\n\nSimultaneously, within domestic Canadian airspace, passengers are protected by the **Air Passenger Protection Regulations (APAPR)**. Understanding where international treaty law supersedes domestic statutes, how **Special Drawing Rights (SDR)** are calculated, and how courts define \"safety-related delays\" is critical for airline passengers and industry legal counsel alike. For immediate flight status monitoring, check our [live departures board](/departures).\n\n---\n\n### The Montreal Convention 1999: The International Gold Standard\n\nThe Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules for International Carriage by Air (Montreal Convention 1999) was drafted under the auspices of ICAO and ratified by over 140 nations, including Canada, the United States, all EU member states, and East Asian jurisdictions.\n\n#### The Principle of Treaty Exclusivity:\nUnder the landmark Supreme Court of Canada precedent (*Thibodeau v. Air Canada, 2014 SCC 67*), the Montreal Convention provides the **exclusive cause of action** for claims arising out of international carriage. Domestic provincial consumer protection acts or common law torts cannot override or supplement the treaty’s limits!\n\n```\nInternational Flight Itinerary (e.g. YUL -> Paris CDG):\nMONTREAL CONVENTION 1999 GOVERNS EXCLUSIVELY\n[ Article 17: Passenger Injury / Baggage Loss ]\n[ Article 19: Delay of Passenger or Cargo ]\n[ Article 22: Strict Liability Limits in SDR ]\n\nPurely Domestic Flight Itinerary (e.g. YVR -> Calgary YYC):\nCANADIAN APAPR & AIRLINE TARIFF REGULATIONS GOVERN\n```\n\n---\n\n### Decoding Special Drawing Rights (SDR): The Global Aviation Currency\n\nThe Montreal Convention does not express monetary liability in Canadian Dollars, US Dollars, or Euros. Because national currencies fluctuate and experience inflation, the treaty expresses limits in **Special Drawing Rights (SDR)**—an international reserve asset created by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) based on a weighted currency basket (USD, EUR, CNY, JPY, GBP).\n\nUnder Article 24 of the Convention, ICAO reviews liability limits every five years to account for inflation. The current certified liability caps are:\n\n| Claim Category | Treaty Article | Maximum Liability Limit (SDR) | Approximate CAD Value (~$1.85 CAD / SDR) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Checked & Cabin Baggage** | Article 17(2) / 22(2) | **1,288 SDR** | **~$2,380 CAD** per passenger |\n| **Flight Delay Damages** | Article 19 / 22(1) | **5,346 SDR** | **~$9,890 CAD** per passenger |\n| **Passenger Injury / Death** | Article 17(1) / 21 | **128,821 SDR (Strict Liability)** | **~$238,000 CAD** (Uncapped for airline fault) |\n| **Cargo Loss per Kilogram** | Article 22(3) | **22 SDR / kg** | **~$40.70 CAD / kg** |\n\n*Crucial Legal Distinction:* The 1,288 SDR baggage cap is not an automatic cash payout. The passenger must prove actual economic loss by submitting receipts, proof of purchase, and original baggage tags within strict treaty notice deadlines.\n\n---\n\n### Strict Notice Deadlines: The Fatal Pitfall for Passenger Claims\n\nUnder Article 31 of the Montreal Convention, if a passenger fails to submit written notice within rigid statutory windows, their legal claim is **completely extinguished**—no court in Canada has the equitable jurisdiction to extend the deadline:\n\n1. **Damaged Checked Luggage:** Written notice must be submitted to the airline within **7 calendar days** of receiving the damaged bag.\n2. **Delayed Baggage:** Written notice for expenses incurred (clothing, toiletries) must be submitted within **21 calendar days** of the date the bag was returned.\n3. **Lost Baggage (Presumed Lost):** If a bag has not arrived after **21 calendar days**, it is legally deemed lost forever. The passenger can immediately claim the full 1,288 SDR value.\n4. **Court Action Prescription Period:** Any lawsuit against an airline under the Montreal Convention must be filed in court within **two years** of arrival date.\n\n---\n\n### Canadian APAPR: Cash Delay Compensation vs Montreal Article 19\n\nWhere travelers often experience confusion is the intersection between the Canadian **Air Passenger Protection Regulations (APAPR)** and the Montreal Convention:\n\n```\nCanadian APAPR Focus:       Flat-Rate Statutory Cash Penalty for Time Lost ($400 - $1,000 CAD)\nMontreal Convention Focus:  Reimbursement of Actual Proven Out-of-Pocket Economic Damages\n```\n\nUnder APAPR (administered by the Canadian Transportation Agency):\n- **Large Airlines (Air Canada, WestJet):** Delays over 3 hours: $400 CAD; over 6 hours: $700 CAD; over 9 hours: $1,000 CAD.\n- **The \"Safety Exception\" Defense:** Airlines are legally exempt from paying APAPR statutory cash compensation if the delay was caused by weather, air traffic control ground delay programs, or **unscheduled maintenance required for safety reasons**.\n\nHowever, under **Article 19 of the Montreal Convention**, the legal test is vastly more demanding for the carrier! The airline is strictly liable for provable delay expenses (hotel stays, missed non-refundable cruises, lost business meetings) unless it can prove that it and its servants took *\"all measures that could reasonably be required to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take such measures.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Landmark Precedents: CTA and Federal Court of Appeal\n\nIn recent landmark decisions (*Air Passenger Rights v. Canada (Attorney General), 2022 FCA 225*), the Federal Court of Appeal scrutinized airline tariffs, ruling that carriers cannot use generic boilerplate terms like \"crew shortage\" or \"operational issues\" to circumvent passenger compensation mandates. If an airline cancels a flight due to routine maintenance within its control, it must provide passenger meal vouchers, alternate hotel accommodations, and statutory compensation.\n\nUnderstanding your dual protections under international treaty law and Canadian federal regulations ensures that whenever disruptions occur across global flight corridors, your legal and financial rights remain ironclad."
